item.text:
{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Roles are configuration-allocated relations over steps, not actor kinds or a fixed inventory. Where the corresponding operations occur, the constructor owns bounded implementation and self-review, the assessor independently evaluates a frozen candidate, and the decision owner disposes. Other responsibilities may be allocated.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":627,
          "quote":"Roles are relations over steps that a configuration allocates\n(Configuration above), not kinds of actor and not a fixed inventory.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F1.%20The%20step%2FRoles%20and%20delegation",
          "start_line":621
        }
      ],
      "key":"step_roles",
      "label":"Roles over steps"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A bounded operation grant can cover a declared construction, status and checkpoint sequence. A bounded proxy is a delegate whose ability to accept and advance routine actions must be declared by the governing authority relation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":638,
          "quote":"The governing authority relation declares whether a bounded proxy may\naccept and advance routine actions.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F1.%20The%20step%2FRoles%20and%20delegation",
          "start_line":637
        },
        {
          "end_line":682,
          "quote":"One bounded operation grant may cover its declared construction, status and\ncheckpoint sequence.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F1.%20The%20step%2FRoles%20and%20delegation",
          "start_line":681
        }
      ],
      "key":"bounded_operation_grant",
      "label":"Bounded operation grant and proxy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Context construction is the source-defined procedure joining material closure, projection, envelope fit and exact input acquisition. Z contains the intake's subject, outcome and affected relations; R contains dependency relations whose change can alter result, basis validity, evidence sufficiency or authority; B is the actor's envelope.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":698,
          "quote":"Given a seed set Z (the subject, outcome and affected relations named in the\nintake), a relation family R (the dependency relations whose change can\nalter the result, basis validity, evidence sufficiency or authority), and\nthe actor's envelope B:",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F2.%20Context%20construction%20under%20an%20envelope",
          "start_line":695
        }
      ],
      "key":"context_construction",
      "label":"Context construction under an envelope"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Acquisition is read-only observation of the frame, actual subject and declared inputs needed by a step, within the inputs its intake permits. Activation selects one capable frame or declared active frame configuration and binds reusable declarations to the exact subject and basis. Evaluation applies the activated frame's invariants, evidence rules and result algebra.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":780,
          "quote":"Acquisition. Acquisition is the read-only observation of the material a\nstep needs (its frame, its subject and its declared inputs) within the\ninputs its intake permits it to acquire (§1 Intake).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F2.%20Context%20construction%20under%20an%20envelope",
          "start_line":776
        },
        {
          "end_line":819,
          "quote":"Activation\nbinds each reusable frame declaration to the exact subject and basis and\nloads the declared material relations and evidence sources",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F2.%20Context%20construction%20under%20an%20envelope",
          "start_line":817
        },
        {
          "end_line":830,
          "quote":"Evaluation. The evaluator applies the activated frame's declared\ninvariants, evidence rules and result algebra.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F2.%20Context%20construction%20under%20an%20envelope",
          "start_line":829
        }
      ],
      "key":"acquisition_activation_evaluation",
      "label":"Acquisition, activation and evaluation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A derived surface copies, projects or compresses owning sources, names those sources and owns no determination. A task constraint surface from a current a_c interpretation is an example, subject to the cited constraint-surface law. Currentness is relative to an exact selected basis; it is distinct from fidelity and question-specific sufficiency.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":784,
          "quote":"A derived surface is material derived from owning\nsources by copying, projection or compression, such as a projected frame\nfor a task; it names its sources and owns no determination.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F2.%20Context%20construction%20under%20an%20envelope",
          "start_line":782
        },
        {
          "end_line":901,
          "quote":"They establish neither its fidelity to them nor its\n  sufficiency for a question",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F2.%20Context%20construction%20under%20an%20envelope%2FStaleness%20of%20derived%20surfaces",
          "start_line":899
        }
      ],
      "key":"derived_surface",
      "label":"Derived surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A prompt is a rendered contract over typed authority, not source law; it projects the material selected by acquisition.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":845,
          "quote":"Prompt construction. A prompt is rendered contract over typed authority;\nit is not source law.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F2.%20Context%20construction%20under%20an%20envelope",
          "start_line":844
        }
      ],
      "key":"rendered_prompt",
      "label":"Prompt as rendered contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The source describes five refinements of its core result algebra: admission and activation asks whether a step may begin; construction availability asks whether a candidate is available; disposition asks whether a candidate is accepted at its declared boundary; ambiguity outcome asks whether a declared ambiguity is resolved on the exact basis; effects asks whether an intended effect occurred. These answer different questions from the step's evaluations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":941,
          "quote":"| admission and activation | may the step begin | the step is lawfully admitted and activated on the exact subject and basis |\n| construction availability | did the step yield its output | a candidate is available |\n| disposition | what the decision owner does with an assessed candidate | the candidate is accepted at its declared boundary |\n| ambiguity outcome | what happened to a declared ambiguity | the declared ambiguity is resolved on the exact basis |\n| effects | did an intended external effect occur | the intended effect occurred |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F3.%20Step%20results%20and%20disposition",
          "start_line":937
        }
      ],
      "key":"step_result_refinements",
      "label":"Five predicate-bound result refinements"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Admission and activation outcomes concern lawful admission and activation on the exact subject and basis. A step exists only after its contract is admitted and its frame activated. Named outcomes are activated, admission_refused(authority), admission_refused(basis), closure_unresolved, out_of_frame and cause-qualified activation_refusal.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":953,
          "quote":"A step exists only after its contract is admitted (§1, Admission) and its\nframe is activated on the exact subject and basis (§2).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F3.%20Step%20results%20and%20disposition%2FAdmission%20and%20activation",
          "start_line":949
        }
      ],
      "key":"admission_activation_outcomes",
      "label":"Admission and activation outcomes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["Lawful activation has occurred."],
      "definition": "Construction availability records existence of a step's candidate output: a constructing step's constructed subject or an assessing step's closed results. Its values are candidate_ready, refused, incomplete, re_entry_requested(authority) and re_entry_requested(basis). Output remains a candidate until evaluated.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":986,
          "quote":"A step's output is a candidate until evaluated (FOUNDATIONS §3, P0): the\nconstructed subject of a constructing step, or the closed results of an\nassessing step. Construction availability records whether that output\nexists (P5, P6):",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F3.%20Step%20results%20and%20disposition%2FConstruction%20availability",
          "start_line":983
        }
      ],
      "key":"construction_availability",
      "label":"Construction availability"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A decision owner disposes an assessed candidate."],
      "definition": "Disposition is the acceptance result, distinct from availability, assessment and an assessor's recommendation. Its four semantic values are accept, local_repair, re_enter and reject, all mapped for acceptance at the candidate's declared boundary.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1037,
          "quote":"The disposition is the\nacceptance result: it consumes the candidate's availability and assessment\nresults and stays separate from both (FOUNDATIONS §4 Results).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F3.%20Step%20results%20and%20disposition%2FDisposition",
          "start_line":1033
        }
      ],
      "key":"candidate_disposition",
      "label":"Candidate disposition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A declared ambiguity is being handled."],
      "definition": "Ambiguity outcomes are resolved, carried, escalated and blocked. They answer whether the declared ambiguity is resolved on the exact basis and retain their domain-specific information beside their core value.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1073,
          "quote":"A declared ambiguity is resolved, carried, escalated or blocked, and the\noutcome is recorded either way",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F3.%20Step%20results%20and%20disposition%2FAmbiguity%20outcome",
          "start_line":1068
        }
      ],
      "key":"ambiguity_outcome",
      "label":"Ambiguity outcome"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["An effectful step has an intended external effect."],
      "definition": "Effect outcomes concern attainment of the declared intended outcome: completed, not_effected or completion_unknown. Whether the effect has ended is a separate question, required conjunctively for completed and otherwise recorded as an unresolved end when applicable. Uncertain completion includes completion_unknown and not_effected with an unestablished end.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1099,
          "quote":"An effectful step's intended external effect has one of three values,\nmapped for the predicate \"the intended effect occurred\", that is, the\ndeclared outcome of the effect is attained.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F3.%20Step%20results%20and%20disposition%2FEffects",
          "start_line":1095
        },
        {
          "end_line":1130,
          "quote":"An effect of uncertain completion is one whose value is\n`completion_unknown`, or whose value is `not_effected` with its end not\nestablished.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F3.%20Step%20results%20and%20disposition%2FEffects",
          "start_line":1128
        }
      ],
      "key":"external_effect_outcome",
      "label":"External effect outcome and end"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Total handling declares a next move for every admitted condition class, including an explicit unknown class. It does not assert recognition of every external condition, provider termination or completion of every request. A hanging call, unsatisfiable demand and lost post-effect response remain distinct cases.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1152,
          "quote":"Each admitted condition class has a declared\nnext move, including an explicit unknown class. Total handling does not\nclaim that every external condition is recognised, that an invoked provider\nterminates, or that every request can be completed.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F4.%20Totality%20and%20refinement",
          "start_line":1147
        }
      ],
      "key":"total_handling",
      "label":"Total handling and reconfiguration"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Refinement decomposes an outer transformation T: A → X with success predicates P₀ … Pₙ into steps. A rewrite replaces a representation, step or composite by another claimed to keep its meaning, including partitioning, regrouping, reordering, merging or substitution. Exact steps preserve meaning under the subject's declared interpretation; approximate steps instead require an adequacy relation and a composite end-to-end bound.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1187,
          "quote":"An outer transformation T: A → X with success predicates P₀ … Pₙ\nis faithfully refined by steps Tᵢ",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F4.%20Totality%20and%20refinement%2FRefinement",
          "start_line":1183
        },
        {
          "end_line":1213,
          "quote":"A rewrite step replaces a representation, a step or\na composite by another that is claimed to keep its meaning",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F4.%20Totality%20and%20refinement%2FRefinement",
          "start_line":1211
        }
      ],
      "key":"refinement_and_rewrite",
      "label":"Refinement, rewrite, exactness and approximation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A material transition is proposed for admission."],
      "definition": "The complete enclosing admission relation includes every participant and equality join whose identity, validity, authority, currentness or transition can affect or be affected by admission, including relevant far sides of crossing seams. Each admission owner independently derives and validates it from authoritative inputs.",
      "exceptions": ["A crossing seam's far side may be excluded when authoritative evidence establishes that it cannot participate."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1267,
          "quote":"For a material transition, each admission owner is independently\nresponsible for deriving and validating the complete enclosing relation\nfrom authoritative inputs (P4b, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F4.%20Totality%20and%20refinement%2FComplete%20enclosing-relation%20admission",
          "start_line":1259
        }
      ],
      "key":"complete_enclosing_admission",
      "label":"Complete enclosing-relation admission"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["An admission owner and supported boundary are declared by accepted design."],
      "definition": "Standing structural qualification instantiates the admission law for every such owner and boundary, with case applicability determined by supported inputs, state and operating or threat model. It examines direct, composite or nested, forged, ambiguous, stale, incoherent, competing-authority, reconstructed, replay and atomic-publication-failure forms.",
      "exceptions": ["Unsupported forms require a design-grounded not_applicable disposition and no weaker or competing path."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1317,
          "quote":"Qualification of the admission law is instantiated for every admission\nowner and supported boundary declared by accepted design (P6). Case applicability\nfollows the supported input, state and operating or threat model.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F4.%20Totality%20and%20refinement%2FStanding%20structural%20qualification",
          "start_line":1311
        }
      ],
      "key":"standing_admission_qualification",
      "label":"Standing structural qualification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Work state is an object comprising the current persistent workspace, governing basis, applicable closed results and judgments with invalidators, residuals and known uncertainty over external effects. Resumption re-enters the same method from current material state, possibly with another actor. Checkpointing, resumption and progress accounting are carrier-independent procedures.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1350,
          "quote":"Work state is the tuple of: the current persistent workspace; the governing\nbasis; the applicable closed results and judgments with their invalidators;\nthe residuals; and the known uncertainty over external effects.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F5.%20Work%20state%20and%20restart",
          "start_line":1343
        }
      ],
      "key":"work_state",
      "label":"Work state, resumption and checkpoint"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Progress is accepted material movement toward the selected outcome. Prerequisite work names the obligation enabled; preservation and repeated proof are evidence without new progress; rejected or superseded construction is churn; discovering hidden distance revises the forecast without being negative progress. These are semantic categories, not a numeric progress algorithm.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1382,
          "quote":"Progress is accepted material movement toward the selected outcome;\nprerequisite work names the obligation it enables; preservation and\nrepeated proof are evidence, not new progress; rejected or superseded\nconstruction is churn; discovering previously hidden distance revises the\nforecast and is not negative progress.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F5.%20Work%20state%20and%20restart",
          "start_line":1375
        }
      ],
      "key":"progress_categories",
      "label":"Progress categories"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The persistent workspace and authoritative inputs are the work's continuing truth surface. An actor's context is its bounded, revisable and disposable account, not a second truth surface. Later intake reconstructs governing relations without the prior actor's private context.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1440,
          "quote":"The workspace and its authoritative inputs persist across activations and\nare the continuing truth surface of the work: the surface whose current\nmaterial state decides what the work has and has not done.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F5.%20Work%20state%20and%20restart%2FThe%20workspace%20and%20an%20actor%27s%20account",
          "start_line":1431
        }
      ],
      "key":"workspace_and_actor_account",
      "label":"Workspace truth surface and actor account"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Proportionality relates semantic ambiguity removed to effective reasoning complexity introduced. Complexity includes independent concepts and authority surfaces, cross-document joins, exceptions, branching, states, transitions, reconciled duplicates and decision-relevant detail; it is not raw lines, artifact count or context-window size.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1506,
          "quote":"Proportionality is the relation between semantic ambiguity removed and\neffective reasoning complexity introduced.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F6.%20Cost%20and%20proportionality",
          "start_line":1492
        }
      ],
      "key":"proportionality",
      "label":"Proportionality"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A cost claim is the governed question."],
      "definition": "A selected cost claim declares an allowance and reports spend measured under the selected execution binding or explicitly unmeasured. Material startup, framework processing, external computation, transport and assurance costs are distinct components. Transferred-context cost includes every delegated actor.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1552,
          "quote":"Cost is selected, not universal. Where a cost claim is the governed\nquestion, the activation declares its allowance and reports its spend,\nmeasured under the selected execution binding or recorded as unmeasured",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F6.%20Cost%20and%20proportionality",
          "start_line":1549
        }
      ],
      "key":"selected_cost_claim",
      "label":"Selected cost claim"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A resource limit bounds context, attention, wall time, spend, human effort, communication or machine resources consumed by activation. Its intake, capability envelope or selected execution binding must declare it. This law supplies no numerical limit; the selected execution binding supplies values and measurable units and boundaries.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1603,
          "quote":"A resource limit bounds a resource that an activation consumes: context,\nattention, wall time, spend, human effort, communication or a machine\nresource (P0). This subsection owns the law for every such limit. It sets\nno value",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F6.%20Cost%20and%20proportionality%2FResource%20limits",
          "start_line":1600
        }
      ],
      "key":"resource_limit",
      "label":"Declared resource limit"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A closed result identifies frame and activation, subject, basis, evaluation, actor, evidence, verdict, residual uncertainty and invalidation conditions. Its reuse depends on exact applicability and three invalidator kinds: material input change, changed basis and a declared revising observation that occurs.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1657,
          "quote":"A closed result identifies the frame and its activation, the subject, the\nbasis, the evaluation, the actor, the evidence, the verdict, the residual\nuncertainty and the invalidation conditions",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F7.%20Closed%20result%20and%20reuse",
          "start_line":1654
        }
      ],
      "key":"closed_result",
      "label":"Closed result and invalidators"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Transition evidence is a consumer-represented read model identifying exact candidate, affected relations, changed realisation paths, added or removed authority paths, retained and open seams, focused and required integration proof, explicit non-changes, any activated assessor's recommendation and the owner's disposition once made. It is not specification, design or admission authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1725,
          "quote":"Consumers\nchoose the representation. Transition evidence is a read\nmodel; it does not become specification, design or admission authority",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F7.%20Closed%20result%20and%20reuse%2FTransition%20evidence",
          "start_line":1722
        }
      ],
      "key":"transition_evidence",
      "label":"Transition evidence"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "ASSURANCE is presented as a tier-2 owner of assurance and testing law: verification layers; testing taxonomy, proof authorities and portability; test authority; scenario bundles; installed development proof; significant paths; product testing frames; combinatorial coverage and testing value; legitimate-path and technical-debt erasure; evidence composition; subject acquisition; counterexample localisation; and interface integration. Its family table is F14. It consumes tier-1 laws without adding tier-1 definitions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":11,
          "quote":"Tier 2. Owns the assurance and testing law of the method",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE",
          "start_line":3
        },
        {
          "end_line":20,
          "quote":"adds no definition to tier 1.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE",
          "start_line":20
        }
      ],
      "key":"assurance_member",
      "label":"ASSURANCE document role"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The verification layers preserve distinct truths: requirements preserve invariant truth and ask what must be true; design preserves structural choice and asks how that truth is realised; scenarios preserve operational meaning and ask whether the realised system can do the claimed thing. Scenarios are concrete end-to-end product-owner use cases validating intent through realised behaviour.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":40,
          "quote":"| Requirements | invariant truth | what must be true? |\n| Design | structural choice | how is that truth realised? |\n| Scenarios | operational meaning | can the realised system actually do the claimed thing? |",
