      "key":"effect_evidence_and_end_mapping",
      "statement":"Use admitted evidence about the subject. completed → satisfied requires full intended effect and established end. not_effected → falsified requires evidence that the declared outcome is unattained and no later completion can attain it: examples include no effect beginning, an effect ending short, a bounded interval closing partly undone, or prior action destroying what the outcome needs; name any part that occurred. completion_unknown → indeterminate when neither other value is established, including lost response without observed counterexample or full effect observed while it may still act; name the deciding observation. Lost response, absent record or no context trace is not evidence of not_effected. End is established by subject evidence that action ceased or never began; if an effect began without established end, record the needed observation separately. End does not decide not_effected: a closed incomplete interval can falsify outcome while action continues. Counterexample and end residual remain independent. Before repetition or dependent action, reconcile uncertain completion sufficiently for that action; not_effected with unknown end licenses neither absence assumptions nor repetition.",
