      "key":"resumption_checkpoint_and_progress_rules",
      "statement":"Resumption recovers applicable progress, checks every reused result's invalidators, reopens only affected support and reconciles uncertain effects before repetition/dependent action sufficiently for that action. It implies no idempotence: lawful next moves for uncertain effects are reconciliation, yield or stop, never blind repetition. Acceptance binds one exact checkpoint and does not enlarge outcome or affected relations; the next already-authorised bounded action needs no renewed human approval. A preservation checkpoint under an exact grant names parent/predecessor, included territory, concurrent-writer disposition and durable evidence location; it is neither acceptance, promotion nor publication. Restart from less than retained work states why repair is insufficient, surviving obligations/evidence and changed causal assumptions; reconstructability alone does not choose restart. Counts of paperwork, code, tests, commits or reviews alone are not progress. Finer categories must name and refine this owner, mapping each to progress, prerequisite work, non-progress evidence, churn or forecast revision; an unmappable category requires owner change. Execution protocol is false for unreconstructable context, constructor reports replacing required independent live evidence, proxy overreach, unrelated incompleteness blocking a closed cone, same-process proof of materially cross-process authority or rejected activity reported as accepted progress.",
