      "key":"proportionality_and_cost_requirements",
      "statement":"Each constraint identifies removed semantic ambiguity and justifies its added reasoning complexity through sufficient contraction of admissible interpretations. Rival authority, failure-classification uncertainty and evidence uncertainty count only as material alternative interpretations, not independent benefits. Greater capability may enlarge the bounded relation resolvable but grants no authority, weakens no invariant and waives no acceptance. Detailed symbolic design is proportional when one bounded model within capacity resolves material alternatives; divergent implementation paths, states, tests, reviews and joins are counterfactual evidence of those alternatives, not another numerator. A prior design removing no material alternative is disproportionate, as are additions duplicating truth, adding reconciliation or expanding reasoning without materially reducing ambiguity. Existing obligations should absorb detail where ownership and meaning survive. Repeated acquisition, assembly, hashing, preparation, status, review and evidence generation require an identified change, decision or assurance need; repetition adds neither validity nor independence, reuse remains conditional, and materially outcome-affecting operational assumptions require cited UP-019 qualification. For governed cost claims, declare allowance, report measured or unmeasured spend, separate concealable cost components, and declare supported dimensions/workloads before qualification. Report volume is not model traffic. No universal quota, ticket/actor count or separate accounting carrier follows.",
